Here are the main reasons you’ll eventually run into trouble with a naïve  
“deepClone” based on JSON and what you can do about it:

1. It only handles JSON-serializable data  
   • Functions, class instances, Date-objects, RegExp, Map/Set, TypedArrays, BigInt, Symbol, undefined, Infinity/–Infinity/NaN all get lost or mangled.  
   • Dates become ISO-strings, RegExps become plain `{}`, `undefined` and `Symbol` disappear, non-JSON numbers turn into `null`.  
   → If you need to preserve types beyond the JSON subset, JSON-based cloning simply won’t work.

2. It blows up on circular references  
   • `JSON.stringify({ a: {}.a = obj })` will throw “Converting circular structure to JSON.”  
   • In many real world trees/graphs you end up with cycles or shared sub-objects and you can’t even clone them.

3. You lose prototypes and property descriptors  
   • Everything comes back as a plain object (`{}`). You never get methods on your original prototype, inherited getters, non-enumerable properties or accessors back.  
   → You end up with a shallow plain object, not a true clone with the same prototype chain.

4. JSON.stringify can invoke user code  
   • If any object in the graph has a custom `toJSON()` method, you trigger that code (possibly side-effects!)  
   → This can leak internal state or change things you didn’t expect just by cloning.

5. Security & performance considerations  
   • A maliciously large or deeply nested object can exhaust memory or stack while `JSON.stringify`/`parse` runs (DoS).  
   • Special keys like `"__proto__"` can be used for prototype-pollution attacks if you later merge or assign the cloned object uncritically.

What to do instead
------------------

1. Use the built-in structuredClone (modern browsers, Node 17+)  
   ```js
   // Preserves Dates, RegExps, Maps, Sets, ArrayBuffers, TypedArrays, 
   // handles circulars, keeps prototype descriptors intakt, 
   // and is memory-safe vs. JSON.stringify
   function deepClone(obj) {
     return structuredClone(obj);
   }
   ```

2. If you need wider support or more control, pull in a battle-tested library  
   • Lodash’s `_.cloneDeep`  
   • rfdc (Really Fast Deep Clone)  
   • immer (for immutable patterns)  
   These handle cycles, most built-in types, and have options for customizers.

3. Roll your own specialized clone (only if you really need custom behavior)  
   – Use a `WeakMap` to track seen objects and handle cycles.  
   – Detect each type via `Object.prototype.toString.call(obj)` or constructor checks (`obj instanceof Date`, etc.).  
   – Recreate the target object with the same prototype:  
     ```js
     const proto = Object.getPrototypeOf(obj);
     clone = Object.create(proto);
     ```  
   – Copy over property descriptors (enumerable, writable, getters/setters) with `Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptors` + `Object.defineProperties`.  
   – Recursively clone each own key (including `Symbol` keys).  

   Example sketch:
   ```js
   function deepClone(obj, seen = new WeakMap()) {
     if (obj === null || typeof obj !== 'object') {
       return obj;
     }
     if (seen.has(obj)) {
       return seen.get(obj);
     }
     // Handle built-ins early
     if (obj instanceof Date)    return new Date(obj);
     if (obj instanceof RegExp)  return new RegExp(obj);
     if (obj instanceof Map)     {
       const m2 = new Map();
       seen.set(obj, m2);
       for (const [k,v] of obj) m2.set(deepClone(k, seen), deepClone(v, seen));
       return m2;
     }
     if (obj instanceof Set) {
       const s2 = new Set();
       seen.set(obj, s2);
       for (const v of obj) s2.add(deepClone(v, seen));
       return s2;
     }
     // TypedArray, ArrayBuffer, etc. you’d handle here…
     // Default object/array
     const proto = Object.getPrototypeOf(obj);
     const copy = Array.isArray(obj) ? [] : Object.create(proto);
     seen.set(obj, copy);
     const descriptors = Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptors(obj);
     for (const [key, desc] of Object.entries(descriptors)) {
       if ('value' in desc) {
         desc.value = deepClone(desc.value, seen);
       }
       Object.defineProperty(copy, key, desc);
     }
     // Handle Symbol-keyed properties if you need them
     return copy;
   }
   ```

Bottom line: `JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(x))` is only ever safe for trivial,  
flat, JSON-style data. As soon as you cross over into real JS types,  
circular graphs or prototype inheritance, you’ll need a proper cloning strategy.
